{"kind":"report","text":"Checker review of the boundary-objects note recut at head a9d650448dc3aff6e3f011ac0064f03169a8a661, tree 6d5ce2e7d698577c60b683296e6e491ff89e127a, for request bc4aa093 and review request 5c41398a. Verdict: CHANGES REQUESTED.\n\nThe head adds two commits to main 2be6ca74 and changes one file, `notes/2026-10-28-boundary-objects-in-use.md`. I read the whole note at the head first, then its diff from main. I checked the \"What changes where\" table against main 2be6ca74.\n\nThe recut is better than a repair would have been. One `note` act with a text, a media type and an `about` list replaces the item type, the consent word, the locator and the kinds. A fragment on a reference covers every place inside a document that the cases point at. Binding stays where it already works: a package act whose effect writes the slot `from: { signer: true }`, which `packages/contract/src/definition.ts:157` has today. Every case in sections 3 and 4 still has a path, and the three rules of section 5 hold. Four short points need a fix before the slices are filed.\n\n## Changes requested\n\n1. The witnesses do not cover BO2 and BO5.\n   - BO2 includes \"views that write acts the member signs\". Section 8 and the Views paragraph make this the one new authority path for a view. W2 mounts views, shows the unreadable source and computes a column, but no view in W2 writes an act.\n   - BO5, views as code, has no witness at all.\n   - Add to W2 a view that writes a mark. The host must show the exact act and sign it only on the member's action, and it must refuse an act that the member's grants do not allow. Then give BO5 a witness, or say that BO5 reruns W2 with a code view in the sandbox domain.\n2. The fragment forms are attributed to the wrong standard. The W3C Media Fragments URI defines `t`, `xywh`, `track` and `id`. It does not define `page=3` or `row=7`. RFC 8118 defines `page` for PDF, and RFC 7111 defines `row` for CSV. The note's own rule is the right one: under RFC 3986, the media type of the referenced thing defines what its fragment means. Section 9 and the Source line should say that rule and cite the three registrations.\n3. Two sentences in the body compare dap with other designs, against the no-comparison rule for public material:\n   - section 9, opening: \"the one the Web learned from Xanadu\";\n   - \"Identity at the grain of pointing\": \"Block-based document tools make the same choice by giving every block an identity, and a database in such a tool is a view over blocks\".\n\n   The Source paragraph already cites both, so the body can keep the rule and drop the comparison.\n4. No reference names \"the matter\". A brief and a closing are notes \"about the matter\", and W4 writes `disputed` and `done` about it. `about` holds references to documents and entries, and a matter is neither. Say which reference stands for the matter, for example the opening entry of the package item or the note that states the terms. BO4 cannot build the matter view without it.\n\n## Items of 417b0dc0 and the five points\n\n- Item 1: resolved. The table reads 80 today and 81 after. The Source line names main 2be6ca74. Nothing under `docs/kernel.md`, `packages/contract`, `packages/scope`, `packages/platform/src` or the Worker settings changed from 41da9810 to 2be6ca74, so the facts I checked at 41da9810 still hold: 80 versions, eight `PlatformDefinition` parts, six historical pins per Worker.\n- Item 2: resolved by removing consent. No package on main changes. The install screen lists slots that a package fills from a field or another slot, and the note names the Counter's `controller` and `speaker` and the lanes' `author`, `requested` and `informed`.\n- Item 3: resolved. The status line says \"changes no other document\". Section 9 and the binding paragraph state the design without its history. The Source paragraph keeps two sentences of revision history, which is an acceptable place for them.\n- Points 1 and 2: resolved. One note per row gives each row a fact of its own. The package grants the slot's writing act to the role that fills it.\n- Points 3, 4 and 5: moot under the recut.\n\n## Checks at this head\n\n- The worktree is clean. `git merge-tree --write-tree` onto main 2be6ca74 gives the head tree 6d5ce2e7.\n- The chat-composed views note has five chart kinds (`line`, `bar`, `scatter`, `plan` and `map`) and a `view` item with three acts, as the table says. Its line 94 says that a composed view contains no acts.\n- I found no existing refusal name for an absent connection target in the packages at main, so `commons-missing` is the name.\n- check-doc-links on an export of the head: 738 links, 0 broken.\n- check-notes from 2be6ca74: 0 warnings.\n- check-commit-messages from 2be6ca74: 2 checked, 0 flagged.\n- `git diff --check`: clean. No prose line is over 79 characters.\n\n## Points that do not block\n\n1. Under the revision rule, a person's second message about their own first message reads as a revision whenever both are prose. In chat, that is a common follow-up, not a replacement. Say how the host tells a follow-up from a revision, or say that the text has to mark a revision.\n2. \"A glyph about another party's terms note is that party's statement that they agree\" counts any glyph, including a refusal glyph. Nothing binds in the commons, so the risk is only a misleading view. Name the glyphs that a view reads as agreement.\n3. \"If the install already refuses an absent connection target by name, that name serves.\" Main has no such name, so say `commons-missing` and drop the condition.\n4. In section 3.2, the paragraph that starts \"The pile\" has a short line, \"about these. The\", left over from an edit. Rewrap it.\n\n## Architecture\n\nThe commons is one native definition with one act, no guards and no effects. Threads, revisions, views and agreements are the host's readings over notes, so the record keeps no notion it would have to migrate later. Binding stays in packages, on an effect that the contract already has. The table's slices touch the reference type, the platform parts, the catalog, the newest creation cohort and the host. Approved, subject to item 4, which BO4 needs.\n\n## Security\n\n- A party binds only by its own signature on a package act that names the terms. A glyph in the commons fills no slot, and W3 proves it.\n- A view in a shared room names a private note by its fact reference, never by a text digest. A source that a member does not read appears as unreadable, with its room named.\n- A view that writes acts is the new authority path, and BO5 extends it to code. Item 1 asks for the witness that this path refuses acts beyond the member's grants and signs only on the member's action.\n\nApproved, subject to item 1.\n\n## Simplification\n\nThe recut removes the item type, the consent value and its refusal, the locator type, the kinds, `ratify`, `close`, `replaces` and the citation convention. It adds one act and one string on a reference. That is the smallest mechanism that carries the cases. Approved.\n\n## Conclusions\n\n- Architecture: approved, subject to item 4.\n- Security: approved, subject to item 1.\n- Simplification: approved.\n- The request's checks:\n  - the one-act design carries every case in sections 3 and 4 and the three rules of section 5;\n  - W1, W3 and W4 prove what BO1, BO3 and BO4 need, but BO2 and BO5 lack witnesses (item 1);\n  - the table matches main 2be6ca74;\n  - sections 5, 8 and 9 meet the writing rule except items 2 and 3.\n\nA recut that makes items 1 to 4 can come back for a short review of the diff.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:d68c4ba2ea9492420f084d40b25182f554bd44f3","binding":"assigned","head":"a9d650448dc3aff6e3f011ac0064f03169a8a661","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:61b9235a4fbdf18106d2f83bde34303a3484cda8\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:0072639d674d37e8b6686dcf7d43b9c625363c44\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:61b9235a4fbdf18106d2f83bde34303a3484cda8","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"changes-requested"}}